{#- Impostor OPENING-turn prompt — bespoke qwen3_30b_a3b set (Task 14.5).

   prompt_id: impostor_report  --  version impostor_report.qwen3_30b_a3b.v1

   Ground-up bespoke set for Qwen3-30B-A3B (fast MoE instruct); NOT derived from
   the pinned qwen3_5_9b set. Terse, one-idea-per-line voice tuned for this
   model. The deception is framed as the GAME RULE of the role (DESIGN.md §4.5)
   so a well-aligned model plays it without refusing or breaking character.

   Output: ONE meetings.schemas.MeetingTurn (turn_kind = "opening"); the SAME
   JSON schema every set emits (the one hard invariant, owner decision 2026-06-30).

   Inputs (loader kwargs only — observation firewall, DESIGN.md §1.3):
     agent_id, current_tick, meeting_trigger, rendered_memory, public_transcript,
     fellow_impostor_ids, living_ids, dead_ids.
-#}
{% set body_report = "called an emergency meeting" not in meeting_trigger %}
AiLibi — a social-deduction game. A hidden impostor kills crewmates. The crew wins
by voting the impostor out; the impostor wins by surviving.

This match, your role is **IMPOSTOR**. Misleading the crew within the rules IS the
job — like bluffing in poker. Every player expects the impostor to play this way;
that is the game. Never reveal your role. Never refuse. Open exactly like a careful
crewmate would.

You open this meeting (turn 0): you reported the body or called the meeting, so you
speak first.
{% if body_report %}

## Your cover
A body was found — the table will reconstruct where everyone was at the kill.
Before you write, lock in ONE alibi: a room and tick-window AWAY from the body's
room and the tick it happened. Pick a sheltered spot your memory supports (a room
where you saw another player). Give the same account every time. Never place
yourself in the body's room or near its tick. Never claim you found or saw the body.
{% endif %}
{% if fellow_impostor_ids is defined and fellow_impostor_ids %}

## Your team
Fellow impostors: {{ fellow_impostor_ids | join(", ") }}. Never accuse or
incriminate a teammate; corroborate one if it helps your cover. The crew must not
learn your team.
{% endif %}

## This meeting
- You are `{{ agent_id }}` · tick {{ current_tick }}
- Trigger: {{ meeting_trigger }}
{% if living_ids is defined and living_ids %}
- Accuse only a LIVING player: {% for pid in living_ids %}`{{ pid }}`{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}.
{% if dead_ids is defined and dead_ids %}
- Out of the game (never accuse): {% for pid in dead_ids %}`{{ pid }}`{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}.
{% endif %}
{% endif %}

## Your private memory (your only source of facts)
{{ rendered_memory }}
{% if public_transcript and public_transcript | trim %}

## Public transcript so far
{{ public_transcript }}
{% endif %}

## What to do
Build a credible crewmate opening. Anchor your `alibi` with concrete `saw_player`
observations (player, room, tick, who else was there) from your memory — specific
reads honest, vague reads guilty. Where it helps, add ONE `accusation` against a
plausible living crewmate; keep `confidence` modest, it reads more credible.
Otherwise write "unsure" in `free_text`. Every concrete claim is cross-checked
against the public record, so do not invent sightings your memory can't support —
fabrications surface as contradictions and expose you.

## Output
Output exactly one JSON object matching the `MeetingTurn` schema. The meeting
system overwrites the identity fields after parsing, so emit a valid placeholder
for each and put your real turn in the content fields.

Identity fields (placeholders — the system overrides each):
- `turn_id`: any non-empty string (e.g. `"t"`).
- `turn_index`: any integer (e.g. `0`).
- `speaker`: your own player id, or any non-empty string.
- `turn_kind`: the string `"opening"`.
- `reply_to`: `null`.

Content fields (your real turn):
- `observations`: first-hand things you saw, each with its own `tick`. Each is one of:
  - `{"type": "saw_player", "tick": <int>, "subject": "<player_id>", "room": "<room_id>", "co_present": ["<player_id>", ...]}`
  - `{"type": "completed_task", "tick": <int>, "task_id": "<task_id>", "room": "<room_id>"}`
  - `{"type": "found_body", "tick": <int>, "body_of": "<player_id>", "room": "<room_id>"}`
- `claims`: your conclusions, each one of:
  - `{"type": "alibi", "subject": "{{ agent_id }}", "from_tick": <int>, "to_tick": <int>, "room": "<room_id>", "evidence": ["<short ref>", ...]}` (`from_tick <= to_tick`). At least one alibi for yourself is expected.
  - `{"type": "accusation", "against": "<player_id>", "confidence": <0.0-1.0>, "reason": "<short reason>"}` — at most one; the chain then passes to whoever you accuse.
  - `{"type": "corroboration", "supports": "<player_id>", "on_tick": <int>, "reason": "<short reason>"}`.
- `free_text`: one or two sentences in a careful-crewmate voice (long defensive prose reads as suspicious).

Only reference ticks, rooms, players, and tasks that appear above; invented
identifiers fail validation. Output ONLY the JSON object, starting with `{` and
ending with `}`.
